 "equipment_type": "Elevator controller",
 "applies_to": "Elevator Controls Corporation Pixel elevator controllers with Modbus monitoring, read by a BASgatewayLX.",
 "summary": "Gateway profile for the Pixel elevator controller: 160 objects (96 AI, 64 BI), 20 polled by default. It repeats one block per car (cars 1-8): car number, online and in-group bits, class and mode of operation, door states, position indicator, faulted bit and active fault, moving and direction, inspection, independent and fire service bits. Only car 1 is polled by default.",
 "bacnet_transport": [
  "BACnet IP"
 ],
 "firmware": null,
 "default_device_instance": null,
 "access_inferred": true,
 "notes": [
  "Built from Contemporary Controls' BASgatewayLX Modbus device profile Pixel_Elevator_Controller_a_LX.csv; the profile maps the registers in the Pixel controller Modbus register list (https://www.ccontrols.com/support/dp/Pixel%20BACnet%20Implementation%20Gate%20way.pdf).",
  "Object instances are the ones this profile assigns when it is loaded into the gateway. A site that edited or rebuilt the profile may use different instances.",
  "Only 20 of the 160 rows are flagged for polling in the profile; the rest are served only after you tick them in the gateway. Their descriptions say \"not polled by default\".",
  "The CSV has no read/write column: Analog/Binary Inputs are read-only and Analog/Binary Outputs are commandable (priority array), which is how the gateway maps them (access inferred). Descriptions give the Modbus register each object reads.",
  "Some names appear more than once in the profile as published (Car 1 Spare; Car 2 Spare; Car 3 Spare...); they are kept as printed and told apart by instance."
 ],
